{
  "abstract": "A computer-assisted proof that Dürer's conjecture and the strong and weak flat Dürer conjectures (every convex polyhedron has a net after sufficiently strong squeezing along every, respectively some, axis) are false, by convex polyhedra with triangular faces and no non-overlapping edge unfolding: an explicit rational polyhedron with 276 vertices and 548 triangular faces after every vertical squeeze by a factor at most 2⁻³²⁷⁶⁸; its bottom cap, with 71 vertices and 137 triangular faces, as an arbitrarily flat convex cap with no net; and one explicit polyhedron with 274·2¹⁸⁴ + 2⁹³ + 6 vertices that has no net after a squeeze by any factor 0 < s ≤ 1 along any axis.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Robert D. Barish"
    }
  ],
